From 26421c8c510cc9e0b96ffc4684f15a95fe895d2e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 20:01:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] 3D: the depth of the nearest thing
 measured from two pictures, and of subtitles planned from it

A 3D Blu-ray gives its subtitles a depth for each frame. MV-HEVC (Apple's
spatial video, 3D films for the Vision Pro), side-by-side and
top-and-bottom files, and MVC streams without plane offsets give none, so
Kodi draws their subtitles at a fixed depth, the screen by default. A
thing nearer than that depth, behind the subtitle, is then hidden by
something that the eyes see behind it: a conflict of depth cues that
strains the eyes.

StereoDepth.h/.cpp, which know nothing of Kodi, measure and plan:

- MeasureNearest(): blocks of 8 x 8 of the left eye's picture (a small
  copy, 480 x 270) with texture are looked for along the same rows of
  the right eye's, by the sum of absolute differences less the block
  means (an eye brighter than the other does not move the match), to a
  fraction of a pixel by a parabola through the costs, from 32 pixels in
  front of the screen to 16 behind it. A block whose best match is not
  clearly better than any other two or more away, or leaves too much
  difference, is left out. The result is not the nearest block but the
  one with 1 % of the matched blocks at or nearer than it, and never
  fewer than 4: a block matched wrong, a bright pixel, noise do not move
  it, a thing of a few blocks does.
- SignatureOf()/IsCut(): a cut, by the mean light of 16 x 9 cells.
- CPlanner: a subtitle appears in front of the nearest thing measured in
  its area in the last second (two measures in a row agreeing), by a
  margin of 0.25 % of the width, never nearer than 2 % (a comfort
  limit) and never behind the screen. While it is shown it only comes
  forward, at 4 % of the width a second at most, where two measures in
  a row put something nearer: it never jumps and never goes back. The
  next subtitle within half a second keeps the depth of the one before
  unless its own is 0.15 % or more away. A cut keeps what was measured
  before it until two measures of the new shot were made. The interface
  over the video gets, as it opens, the depth in front of the nearest
  thing of the whole picture, and keeps it while open, coming forward
  only to stay in front of the subtitles.

Measured on a Ryzen 9 7950X3D at -O2 for x86-64: 2.4 ms for one measure
(the subtitles' area and the whole picture), on a thread of its own in
the next commit.
